  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tanzania &#x2013; regenerative spice production</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">With a DGGF Startup financing Trianon Spices B.V. will be able to expand its spice processing facility in Tanzania together with its local subsidiary. Trianon Spices process and export cardamom, cinnamon, black pepper and clove, sourced from more than 1,500 small farmers in the region. Spices are then processed, packed and exported for sale to large European wholesale buyers.Trianon strives to become a world leader in regenerative spice production (meaning: high focus on increasing biodiversity, rotating crops, minimizing chemical input), starting in Tanzania. Trianon plans to expand its customer base to include other regions and higher margin buyers closer to the end consumer. They seek financing to: scale volumes and construct an additional processing facility, improve quality and expand salesThe expected impact is additional local direct employment of approximately 40 FTE and local indirect employment of approximately 3,000 jobs. All employees will be trained and 4,000 local spice farmers will receive good prices for their products and receive technical assistance to improve their farming system on a sustainable way.In 2021 Trianon applied for a DHI (investeringsvoorbereidingsstudie) to get investment ready this helped them to prepare properly for investors like DGGF and Kampani.</narrative>
  </description>
